From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Maciej=20=C5=BBenczykowski?= <maze@google.com>
Date: Thu, 13 May 2021 15:08:38 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: xt_IDLETIMER: add send_nl_msg support
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Let the IDLETIMER target take --send_nl_msg, which Android's netd passes
for every interface idle timer. Such a timer sends a uevent through its
kobject, naming the interface, the state (active or inactive), the boot
time of the change in nanoseconds and the user ID whose packet woke the
interface, when it expires and when a packet starts it again. Its sysfs
file then reads "0 <seconds since expiry>" once it has expired. The
timers also remember when a packet last arrived and correct an expiry
that lapsed during a suspend.

Upstream accepts the argument only for compatibility and refuses it with
EOPNOTSUPP, and netd's idle notifications for mobile data and wifi
depend on the uevents.

Signed-off-by: Maciej Żenczykowski <maze@google.com>
Signed-off-by: Vivekanand Tryambake <quic_vtryamba@quicinc.com>
Signed-off-by: Satya Durga Srinivasu Prabhala <quic_satyap@quicinc.com>
Signed-off-by: Carlos Llamas <cmllamas@google.com>
Signed-off-by: Subash Abhinov Kasiviswanathan <subash.a.kasiviswanathan@oss.qualcomm.com>
(cherry picked from the Android common kernel android17-6.18: the
three commits cf468a86d072, 382e68666b7f and 80af3825b7d4 by Maciej
Zenczykowski that re-add the support, folded with the fixes that follow
them: c6ee14d14edc (unregister_pm_notifier(), by Vivekanand Tryambake,
Satya Durga Srinivasu Prabhala and Carlos Llamas), 770ffb0a835c
(sk_uid(), by Carlos Llamas) and cafbae1761e5 (the full socket check, by
Subash Abhinov Kasiviswanathan). The protocol, the delayed trigger and
the resume handling come from the Android kernels that carried it
before: f0c2df2b1228 (JP Abgrall) and 7ded4359680d (Subash Abhinov
Kasiviswanathan).)
[Adapted to 6.18.54: the new CONFIG_NETFILTER_XT_TARGET_IDLETIMER_NL_MSG
keys everything the Android kernel changes. Without it send_nl_msg is
refused as before, a packet restarts the timer as before, and no
notifier is registered, so the Linux product's IDLETIMER is upstream's.
The state each timer keeps for the uevents is set up in one helper
instead of in both create functions, and a failed notifier registration
is a pr_warn(). The uapi comment on send_nl_msg no longer says unused.]

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
